1. 4DFlowNet
------------
NeuroFlow is derived from 4DFlowNet, from which it inherits the SR4DFlowNet
architecture, the patch-based training strategy, and the loss formulation.

    Copyright (c) 2020 Edward Ferdian
    License: MIT
    Source:  https://github.com/EdwardFerdian/4DFlowNet

    Ferdian E., Suinesiaputra A., Dubowitz D.J., Zhao D., Wang A., Cowan B.,
    Young A.A. "4DFlowNet: Super-Resolution 4D Flow MRI Using Deep Learning and
    Computational Fluid Dynamics." Frontiers in Physics, 8:138, 2020.

Subsequent cerebrovascular adaptations follow:

    Ferdian E. et al. "Cerebrovascular Super-Resolution 4D Flow MRI."
    bioRxiv 2021.08.25.457611.


2. nnU-Net v2
-------------
Used for Circle-of-Willis segmentation. NeuroFlow calls it for inference only
(nnUNetPredictor, ensemble_folders). It was previously vendored into this
repository; it is now a pinned dependency (nnunetv2==2.5.1).

    Copyright (c) German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ), Division of Medical
    Image Computing
    License: Apache License 2.0
    Source:  https://github.com/MIC-DKFZ/nnUNet

    Isensee F., Jaeger P.F., Kohl S.A.A., Petersen J., Maier-Hein K.H.
    "nnU-Net: a self-configuring method for deep learning-based biomedical
    image segmentation." Nature Methods, 18:203-211, 2021.

The single local modification retained from the vendored copy is recorded in
patches/nnunetv2-2.5.1-polylr-torch-compat.diff. It is required only to retrain
the segmentation models, not to reproduce NeuroFlow results.


3. TopCoW challenge models
--------------------------
The Circle-of-Willis segmentation ensemble weights originate from the TopCoW
challenge. They are not redistributed in this repository and must be obtained
from the challenge organisers.

    Source: https://topcow23.grand-challenge.org/

    Yang K. et al. "TopCoW: Benchmarking Topology-Aware Anatomical
    Segmentation of the Circle of Willis for CTA and MRA." 2023.


